1
00:00:10,299 --> 00:00:12,566
[man on radio] The forecast,
sunshine through today

2
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:14,899
with seasonal temperatures.
We should return--

3
00:00:14,966 --> 00:00:16,599
[up-tempo instrumental music playing]

4
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:24,266
[Jerry]
This is a 1958 Cadillac Eldorado Seville

5
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:26,066
in Gleneagles green,

6
00:00:26,126 --> 00:00:30,196
with a Bimini green metallic interior
covered in protective plastic.

7
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:34,800
It has a 335 horsepower V8 engine.

8
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:39,733
It goes 0 to 60 in 10.7 seconds,
but who cares?

9
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:43,133
The longer people are looking
at you in this car, the better.

10
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:47,500
If you were an American at the top
of whatever you did in the late '50s

11
00:00:47,547 --> 00:00:51,251
and you wanted to drive around
in a mobile version of Las Vegas,

12
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:52,318
this is it.

13
00:00:52,786 --> 00:00:55,655
A car that's as thrilled with itself
as you are.

14
00:00:56,923 --> 00:00:58,925
[phone ringing]

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,300
-[Don] Hello?
-[Jerry] Don? It's Jerry.

16
00:01:01,366 --> 00:01:04,166
[Don] I'm so excited, I'm going
to drop my pants and fire a rocket.

17
00:01:04,798 --> 00:01:07,167
What's the matter, you need money?
What can I do for you?

18
00:01:07,233 --> 00:01:09,102
[Jerry] I'm looking forward
to seeing you today.

19
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:11,533
[Don] You're like a schnorrer
with this car crap.

20
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,233
It's doing a lot to me, you know that.

21
00:01:13,273 --> 00:01:15,575
-[Jerry] Oh, yeah?
-[Don] How about nothing?

22
00:01:20,033 --> 00:01:21,466
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, 

23
00:01:21,514 --> 00:01:25,018
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

24
00:01:25,151 --> 00:01:26,453
That's funny.

25
00:01:26,519 --> 00:01:29,456
[Jerry] My special guest today
is one of the faces that, in my opinion,

26
00:01:29,522 --> 00:01:32,192
would be on the Mount Rushmore
of stand-up comedy...

27
00:01:32,266 --> 00:01:34,066
Mr. Don Rickles.

28
00:01:34,127 --> 00:01:36,496
The other three are Pryor, 
Carlin, and Cosby, by the way.

29
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:38,798
I've known Don for over 20 years,

30
00:01:38,865 --> 00:01:41,701
and as far as I'm concerned,
he is the man.

31
00:01:43,699 --> 00:01:45,033
-[Don] Hello?
-[Jerry] Don?

32
00:01:45,071 --> 00:01:46,339
[Don] What do you mean, "Don"?

33
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:49,733
This isn't a grocery store
for Christ's sake, "Don." [chuckles]

34
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:51,733
-[Jerry] Mr. Rickles?
-[Don] Listen, hurry up,

35
00:01:51,778 --> 00:01:53,880
'cause for the money you're paying me,
this should be over.

36
00:01:53,966 --> 00:01:55,300
Kiss the mezuzah.

37
00:01:55,982 --> 00:01:58,585
-Okay. You don't believe in that? Figures.
-No...

38
00:01:58,966 --> 00:02:00,699
You're one of those voodoo Jews. Okay.

39
00:02:00,753 --> 00:02:02,655
[lively piano music playing]

40
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:05,558
-[Don] You're driving?
-[Jerry] Yes.

41
00:02:05,633 --> 00:02:07,266
[Jerry] I'm gonna drive,
you're gonna jog alongside.

42
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:08,466
Is that all right?

43
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:09,966
Do you have to wear the hat?

44
00:02:10,029 --> 00:02:11,397
-You don't like the hat?
-I wanna see your face.

45
00:02:11,464 --> 00:02:12,465
I paid for Don Rickles.

46
00:02:15,435 --> 00:02:19,806
Take me back to 1958.
Could you afford a car like this in 1958?

47
00:02:19,873 --> 00:02:21,107
No, no. Bicycle.

48
00:02:21,174 --> 00:02:25,211
In those days, my mother, rest her soul,
she had her friend drive me to jobs

49
00:02:25,278 --> 00:02:26,713
and they used to sit in the kitchen

50
00:02:26,799 --> 00:02:28,566
and wait for the boss to come out,
and say,

51
00:02:28,615 --> 00:02:31,150
"We don't want this man here,
he's ruining our business."

52
00:02:31,217 --> 00:02:32,552
My best recollection is

53
00:02:32,619 --> 00:02:34,287
I went to the American Academy
of Dramatic Arts.

54
00:02:34,366 --> 00:02:36,933
-Right. That's not comedy.
-Yeah, I know.

55
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,033
-This is a show about comedy.
-Yeah. Really?

56
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:40,400
[both chuckling]

57
00:02:41,628 --> 00:02:44,297
Who were your friends,
who did you pal around with?

58
00:02:44,364 --> 00:02:47,300
-Guy called Herkie Styles.
-Herkie Styles.

59
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:49,802
-That's a good name for a comic.
-He was a wonderful comedian.

60
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:52,733
-I wasn't much of a hang-around guy...
-Right.

61
00:02:52,799 --> 00:02:54,799
...in the horse room or something.
I never did that.

62
00:02:54,866 --> 00:02:56,033
The where? The horse room?

63
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:57,799
-You know, in Vegas.
-You mean betting.

64
00:02:57,866 --> 00:02:59,766
-Betting, yeah.
-So, you didn't gamble.

65
00:02:59,966 --> 00:03:01,733
-Were you a drinker?
-In those days, I drank

66
00:03:01,781 --> 00:03:04,417
from the side of the mouth,
little drippings of vodka.

67
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:07,086
When you worked those places,
you had to have guts.

68
00:03:07,153 --> 00:03:08,488
-Yes.
-[chuckles]

69
00:03:08,588 --> 00:03:10,290
And a Diet Coke wouldn't do it, you know.

70
00:03:11,899 --> 00:03:14,700
As time marched on,
I never have a drink when I'm not working.

71
00:03:14,766 --> 00:03:16,166
-Never.
-Really?

72
00:03:16,229 --> 00:03:18,598
Except socially, in a restaurant.
At home, never drink.

73
00:03:18,665 --> 00:03:19,999
Then it's not never. You do.

74
00:03:20,066 --> 00:03:22,700
Well, what are you,
from Alcoholics Anonymous?

75
00:03:22,766 --> 00:03:25,066
-I'm trying to find out--
-You're trying to put me in my place.

76
00:03:25,104 --> 00:03:27,373
-I wanna figure out what happened.
-I don't like that.

77
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:30,677
The wife has been trying for a long time.
She keeps moving the medicine.

78
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:34,299
I get up in the morning and say,
"Where's my pills?"

79
00:03:34,347 --> 00:03:35,915
She says, "Find them. Find them."

80
00:03:35,982 --> 00:03:38,451
She made it a scavenger hunt
to stay alive, for crying out loud.

81
00:03:38,518 --> 00:03:40,153
[both laughing]

82
00:03:44,633 --> 00:03:46,566
-[Don] And you know who else I love?
-De Niro.

83
00:03:46,626 --> 00:03:47,827
I worked with him in Casino.

84
00:03:47,894 --> 00:03:50,863
You know what I did? The opening line,
I said... God's truth, Jerry,

85
00:03:50,930 --> 00:03:53,700
with the lights and Scorsese
and everything.

86
00:03:53,766 --> 00:03:57,003
And they go, "Roll them!"
And he goes, [mumbling] "You know...

87
00:03:57,070 --> 00:03:58,404
Charlie... [mumbles] "

88
00:03:58,471 --> 00:04:00,740
I said, "Hold it, I can't work
with this man.

89
00:04:00,807 --> 00:04:03,810
"The man's mumbling and spitting up
all over, I don't need this, Marty."

90
00:04:03,876 --> 00:04:05,211
The crew was on the floor.

91
00:04:05,345 --> 00:04:07,680
They said, "Don't say nothing.
Bob don't like that we get it."

92
00:04:07,747 --> 00:04:10,550
The second day, we were having vodka
in the dressing room every night.

93
00:04:10,950 --> 00:04:12,051
"To Italy." Boom.

94
00:04:13,553 --> 00:04:15,088
[Jerry] But you never have a drink.

95
00:04:18,224 --> 00:04:20,727
I worked for four presidents
of the United States.

96
00:04:20,793 --> 00:04:23,696
Wow. I know the Reagan one.
That was a big night, wasn't it?

97
00:04:23,763 --> 00:04:26,265
That was one of the most exciting nights,
I think, of my career.

98
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:29,369
Sinatra said, "If you don't have
Sir Rickles, you don't have me."

99
00:04:29,435 --> 00:04:30,770
-That's what he said.
-Really?

100
00:04:30,837 --> 00:04:31,871
And Ronald Reagan liked me

101
00:04:31,938 --> 00:04:33,740
'cause when he was governor,
I used to rib him.

102
00:04:33,806 --> 00:04:35,642
I said, "My cousin's going
to the chair Thursday.

103
00:04:36,342 --> 00:04:39,912
One phone call, one call.
Ronnie, you have nothing else to do.

104
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,099
Forget the golf game,

105
00:04:41,147 --> 00:04:43,449
all you have to say is,
'Warden, he's innocent.'"

106
00:04:43,716 --> 00:04:45,118
And he'd fall out of the chair.

107
00:04:45,184 --> 00:04:46,552
[Jerry laughing]

108
00:04:46,633 --> 00:04:48,866
And the big line
was the Billy Graham thing.

109
00:04:49,255 --> 00:04:53,159
"Dr. Graham, if you could fix this hand.
It's making me crazy."

110
00:04:54,027 --> 00:04:58,131
You have to be so much funnier
than most guys to do what you do.

111
00:04:58,197 --> 00:05:01,768
There's no act, no, "You should hear
what Don says about this or that."

112
00:05:01,834 --> 00:05:04,971
You're just using your raw funny
all the time.

113
00:05:05,066 --> 00:05:06,900
-Thank you.
-Which is very difficult to do.

114
00:05:06,939 --> 00:05:07,974
Any comedian knows that.

115
00:05:11,177 --> 00:05:13,313
What was it like playing the Copacabana?

116
00:05:13,379 --> 00:05:16,115
I was handled by a fellow called
Joe Scandore for about 40 years.

117
00:05:16,182 --> 00:05:18,484
-I remember Joe.
-He knew a lot of guys,

118
00:05:18,566 --> 00:05:20,533
-their noses were crooked, you know?
-Yes, I do.

119
00:05:20,586 --> 00:05:23,589
And in those days,
I always got a chance in a nightclub.

120
00:05:23,656 --> 00:05:25,792
They found me funny,
that's how I got to the Copa.

121
00:05:25,858 --> 00:05:28,194
[Don] Everybody that was important came.
Really.

122
00:05:28,494 --> 00:05:29,729
Jules Podell said,

123
00:05:29,800 --> 00:05:32,200
[mimicking Jules] "I don't want this man
in my place. I don't need no wise guy,

124
00:05:32,265 --> 00:05:34,133
no dummy that makes fun of my people.

125
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:36,969
This is high-class, it's the Copacabana,

126
00:05:37,066 --> 00:05:40,766
and this kid will never,
as long as I'm alive, star in this place."

127
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:42,833
And Joe had a high voice, just like,

128
00:05:42,875 --> 00:05:45,712
[high-pitched] "Why? He's a good comedian,
don't you understand?"

129
00:05:45,933 --> 00:05:49,633
[mimicking Jules] "I don't care, Joe,
he's not gonna be in this place."

130
00:05:50,683 --> 00:05:52,952
Two days later,
[mimicking Jules] "Hello, Don,

131
00:05:53,019 --> 00:05:56,356
you're one of the cleverest,
most entertaining guys

132
00:05:56,422 --> 00:05:59,158
and you will star at the Copacabana."

133
00:06:03,900 --> 00:06:05,666
Have you eaten yet today? Anything?

134
00:06:05,765 --> 00:06:07,500
-I had my cornflakes.
-Cornflakes, you had?

135
00:06:07,567 --> 00:06:09,936
-Yeah.
-You really are living in 1958.

136
00:06:10,002 --> 00:06:11,270
-[Don] Yeah.
-What about coffee?

137
00:06:15,241 --> 00:06:17,210
-Yeah.
-But are you in the mood for coffee?

138
00:06:17,276 --> 00:06:20,113
Yeah, what is "in the mood"?
It's America, of course I'll have coffee.

139
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,815
[Jerry] All right,
let's go get some coffee.

140
00:06:27,854 --> 00:06:30,423
-[waiter] Coffee?
-Two coffees, please. Thank you so much.

141
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:31,457
And Sweet'n Low.

142
00:06:31,524 --> 00:06:33,092
Sweet'n Low.
You don't wanna get heavy?

143
00:06:33,159 --> 00:06:34,327
Eighty-seven in May.

144
00:06:34,733 --> 00:06:36,266
Eighty-seven, God bless you.

145
00:06:36,662 --> 00:06:38,798
You know, I keep working.
I gotta keep working.

146
00:06:42,034 --> 00:06:44,704
When I was in the Philippines,
the joke was...

147
00:06:44,799 --> 00:06:46,366
I come out of high school,
"You'll fail..."

148
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:48,700
President of everything
and failing every subject.

149
00:06:48,766 --> 00:06:50,266
So my father, rest his soul, said,

150
00:06:50,333 --> 00:06:51,666
"You're going to Grand Central.

151
00:06:51,733 --> 00:06:53,500
Enlist.
You gotta get outa high school."

152
00:06:53,546 --> 00:06:55,948
And first thing they do,
they take my blood pressure.

153
00:06:56,048 --> 00:06:59,685
You know, 'cause I was a very hyper guy.
A nervous wreck, like I am today.

154
00:06:59,752 --> 00:07:01,888
Okay, look.
[mimicking blood pressure cuff hissing]

155
00:07:04,023 --> 00:07:06,192
-[Jerry laughing]
-"You feelin' all right?"

156
00:07:06,259 --> 00:07:07,760
[laughing]

157
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:09,896
I said, "Yeah, Doc, what's wrong?"
"Oh. Nothing."

158
00:07:10,296 --> 00:07:12,064
-"Let me try to hear."
-[laughing]

159
00:07:12,131 --> 00:07:14,600
I did about four...
[mimicking cuff hissing] You know?

160
00:07:14,666 --> 00:07:16,133
-He said, "Okay."
-[pounding]

161
00:07:16,202 --> 00:07:18,971
I said, "What does that mean?"
"Means you're in the Navy now, son."

162
00:07:19,038 --> 00:07:22,475
I said, "I applied for special service.
I do comedy and jokes."

163
00:07:22,875 --> 00:07:24,944
"Comedy and jokes."

164
00:07:26,245 --> 00:07:27,647
"And where would you like
to be stationed?"

165
00:07:27,713 --> 00:07:31,017
"San Diego, sir. I mean,
there's a great atmosphere to do a show."

166
00:07:31,317 --> 00:07:34,854
"San Diego... Do a show."

167
00:07:35,455 --> 00:07:36,956
Next thing you know, I'm on a ship.

168
00:07:37,023 --> 00:07:39,459
I said, "Where we going?"
"The Philippines."

169
00:07:39,833 --> 00:07:41,233
So you would do shows in the Navy?

170
00:07:41,327 --> 00:07:42,962
Yeah, we had shows on the ship.

171
00:07:43,029 --> 00:07:46,732
At night, they'd put up a platform.
They said, "Rickles is on." You know.

172
00:07:47,533 --> 00:07:49,268
But my joke worked all the time.

173
00:07:49,366 --> 00:07:50,833
"I do comedy."

174
00:07:50,870 --> 00:07:52,338
[chuckling]

175
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:55,174
One guy always kept saying,
"Just keep firing." That's my big joke.

176
00:07:55,266 --> 00:07:57,066
"Keep firing,
don't worry about the comedy.

177
00:07:57,133 --> 00:07:59,066
-We'll talk about that later."
-[both laughing]

178
00:07:59,133 --> 00:08:01,866
And I was on the 20 mm gun.
[mimicking gunshots]

179
00:08:01,948 --> 00:08:04,784
When the smoke cleared, they lift
the canister, put it on the ground.

180
00:08:04,851 --> 00:08:07,587
"Lieutenant, I know... See, I do comedy."

181
00:08:07,653 --> 00:08:09,755
[laughing] I was such a schmuck.

182
00:08:09,889 --> 00:08:12,458
[chuckles] I still thought
that maybe they'd transfer me.

183
00:08:12,533 --> 00:08:14,299
-In the middle of shit...
-To San Diego.

184
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:16,162
Yeah, in the middle of...
[mimicking gunshots]

185
00:08:17,666 --> 00:08:19,900
Bob Newhart and I. Isn't that something?

186
00:08:20,399 --> 00:08:22,835
-Two guys that are completely different.
-Right.

187
00:08:22,933 --> 00:08:26,066
He came from the Midwest.
A real giant gentile, God bless him.

188
00:08:26,105 --> 00:08:27,907
And it's the wives.
I say this on the show.

189
00:08:28,107 --> 00:08:30,209
The wives. You know that with your wife.

190
00:08:30,276 --> 00:08:32,778
If your wife likes the woman
that you're...

191
00:08:32,845 --> 00:08:35,414
And you like the guy,
and she don't like the guy...

192
00:08:35,481 --> 00:08:38,017
-It's over. It's over, am I right?
-That's it.

193
00:08:38,150 --> 00:08:40,219
And I said, "This guy's great, Barbara."

194
00:08:40,286 --> 00:08:42,121
[mimicking Barbara]
"You're not gonna see him."

195
00:08:42,188 --> 00:08:43,489
[both laughing]

196
00:08:44,357 --> 00:08:47,326
Like in Europe,
we used to go into Harrods.

197
00:08:47,393 --> 00:08:49,562
And she'd buy... "I'll get these gloves.

198
00:08:49,633 --> 00:08:53,166
And listen, I'll have that skirt.
Oh, yeah, and that jacket.

199
00:08:53,833 --> 00:08:55,733
Oh, yes, and give me that scarf."

200
00:08:55,801 --> 00:08:58,671
And I'd say,
"Oh, boy, this is a beautiful tie."

201
00:08:58,804 --> 00:09:00,006
"You don't need that."

202
00:09:00,072 --> 00:09:01,574
[both laughing]

203
00:09:02,041 --> 00:09:03,709
That was my name. "You don't need that."

204
00:09:05,945 --> 00:09:09,148
-I know you're a baseball guy.
-Dodgers. I used to go down in the dugout.

205
00:09:09,215 --> 00:09:10,950
Talked to Lasorda.
Put on the uniform one night.

206
00:09:11,617 --> 00:09:14,353
He said, "Go out and take
the pitcher out." I'm in the uniform.

207
00:09:14,420 --> 00:09:17,390
Walk out to the mound. 50,000 people.
"Give me the ball."

208
00:09:17,790 --> 00:09:19,859
[high-pitched] "You don't know me.
What are you doing here?"

209
00:09:20,393 --> 00:09:23,596
Harry Wendelstedt, who passed away,
he was the head umpire, comes racing out.

210
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,633
Rips off the mask, "What the hell...

211
00:09:25,698 --> 00:09:28,000
-Don, can you get me four seats in Vegas,
-[slapping table]

212
00:09:28,067 --> 00:09:29,835
the Dean Martin show?

213
00:09:29,902 --> 00:09:31,070
Four, just four seats."

214
00:09:34,966 --> 00:09:38,100
-Can I get you anything over here?
-How about a picture of China?

215
00:09:38,166 --> 00:09:39,466
[laughing]

216
00:09:39,533 --> 00:09:41,066
-I'll go with you.
-Sarah.

217
00:09:41,113 --> 00:09:42,048
[Sarah] Yes?

218
00:09:42,133 --> 00:09:44,600
-We'll get together, don't say nothing.
-I won't.

219
00:09:45,318 --> 00:09:48,154
Because as soon as I saw you,
I knew. I knew it was you and me.

220
00:09:50,566 --> 00:09:53,500
In those days, it was unheard of
to do what I did.

221
00:09:53,559 --> 00:09:55,461
-Right.
-It was, "Ugh, what is he doing?"

222
00:09:55,528 --> 00:09:59,565
At the beginning, in the audiences,
there was one guy, a wise guy in Montreal,

223
00:10:00,099 --> 00:10:01,767
and he was a bad guy, really bad.

224
00:10:02,066 --> 00:10:04,199
And he was sitting ringside,
and I look at the wife.

225
00:10:04,236 --> 00:10:05,905
I said, "Sir, is that your wife?"

226
00:10:05,972 --> 00:10:07,406
Guy, "Yeah." I said...

227
00:10:07,533 --> 00:10:08,633
"Ooh."

228
00:10:08,674 --> 00:10:10,576
-[both chuckling]
-Isn't that something?

229
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:12,866
Jerry, I said...

230
00:10:14,199 --> 00:10:15,300
"She's a moose."

231
00:10:15,748 --> 00:10:17,550
[both laughing]

232
00:10:17,617 --> 00:10:20,486
Then I turned to the band.
"Am I crazy? You guys take a look."

233
00:10:20,987 --> 00:10:21,954
They all stand up and go,

234
00:10:22,266 --> 00:10:23,199
"Ooh."

235
00:10:23,522 --> 00:10:24,724
[laughing]

236
00:10:24,790 --> 00:10:26,092
-Enjoy your dinner.
-Thanks.

237
00:10:26,158 --> 00:10:27,927
Now I'm gonna watch an old Jew
eat a salad.

238
00:10:27,994 --> 00:10:29,161
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

239
00:10:31,530 --> 00:10:34,634
I was the only guy with Frank Sinatra
to get away with murder.

240
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:35,935
Rest his soul, I loved him.

241
00:10:36,002 --> 00:10:37,870
-Were you guys really friends?
-No.

242
00:10:38,266 --> 00:10:39,633
We're sitting here now, right?

243
00:10:39,699 --> 00:10:42,400
And he would say, "You know..."
I'd say, "Frank, I'm talking."

244
00:10:43,542 --> 00:10:45,311
-Nobody else would do that.
-Nobody.

245
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:46,900
-And he would go...
-[slams table]

246
00:10:46,946 --> 00:10:48,147
-He loved it.
-Oh, loved it.

247
00:10:48,433 --> 00:10:51,133
Give me a night in Vegas,
you're working, he's working...

248
00:10:51,183 --> 00:10:52,752
After the show, you get together?

249
00:10:52,818 --> 00:10:54,253
-Yeah.
-Take me through that.

250
00:10:54,320 --> 00:10:56,789
Usually, I'd have to chase the car.
"Wait up, Frank."

251
00:10:56,866 --> 00:10:58,000
[chuckling]

252
00:10:58,057 --> 00:11:02,194
Whatever he said, we loved.
It was exciting to be with him

253
00:11:02,261 --> 00:11:03,496
-and do what he wanted.
-Why?

254
00:11:03,562 --> 00:11:04,897
He had a charisma that was...

255
00:11:04,964 --> 00:11:06,766
You walked into a restaurant, it stopped.

256
00:11:06,833 --> 00:11:08,699
-It stopped, I'm telling you.
-Right.

257
00:11:09,035 --> 00:11:11,437
But after the shows,
we usually went someplace,

258
00:11:11,504 --> 00:11:13,639
sit with him till 5:00,
6:00 in the morning.

259
00:11:13,706 --> 00:11:15,608
-With drinks. Yeah.
-5:00, 6:00 in the morning?

260
00:11:15,675 --> 00:11:18,444
Then we used to go early in the morning
out to Lake Mead.

261
00:11:18,911 --> 00:11:22,915
I learned how to water-ski.
I got pictures of me on one ski, two skis.

262
00:11:22,982 --> 00:11:24,750
-After staying up all night?
-Yeah.

263
00:11:25,918 --> 00:11:27,586
-Go water-skiing?
-Go water-skiing.

264
00:11:32,324 --> 00:11:34,126
-We're sitting in a hotel...
-Yeah.

265
00:11:34,193 --> 00:11:36,962
...in Monte Carlo, big bay window,
sitting there.

266
00:11:37,063 --> 00:11:38,431
Frank, Tony, myself.

267
00:11:38,497 --> 00:11:39,598
So...

268
00:11:39,999 --> 00:11:41,867
lightning. [mimicking thunderclap]

269
00:11:43,199 --> 00:11:44,266
Frank says, "Don...

270
00:11:45,004 --> 00:11:47,173
You and Julie,
why don't you go outside...

271
00:11:47,807 --> 00:11:49,175
and tell that cameraman...

272
00:11:49,475 --> 00:11:51,410
that photographer,
we're gonna knock him on his ass

273
00:11:51,477 --> 00:11:52,778
-if he keeps taking pictures."
-Right.

274
00:11:53,179 --> 00:11:55,047
Pouring rain and lightning.
It was lightning.

275
00:11:55,114 --> 00:11:56,716
[mimicking thunderclap]
I said, "Well, Frank--"

276
00:11:56,782 --> 00:11:59,018
"Don't out-frank me.
Just go out there now and stop him.

277
00:11:59,100 --> 00:12:01,500
-Put a stop. I want it now."
-Right.

278
00:12:01,754 --> 00:12:03,889
Near the door, I was in the rain
and tuxedo going,

279
00:12:03,956 --> 00:12:06,358
"And don't you start, and you put..."
Talking to nobody.

280
00:12:06,425 --> 00:12:08,160
"And you stop that lightning,
understand me?"

281
00:12:08,461 --> 00:12:09,829
Thank God, the rain stopped.

282
00:12:10,162 --> 00:12:12,765
And we came back in soaking wet
and sat down. He said,

283
00:12:12,832 --> 00:12:14,166
"Thanks guys. You did a good job."

284
00:12:14,233 --> 00:12:15,401
[laughing]

285
00:12:15,768 --> 00:12:16,669
True story.

286
00:12:18,838 --> 00:12:21,507
But I know that if I had more education,

287
00:12:21,841 --> 00:12:24,210
I could've been a damn good psychiatrist.

288
00:12:24,766 --> 00:12:27,500
Really. That's what I do on the stage,
it's a form of psychiatry.

289
00:12:27,566 --> 00:12:29,566
I know, but what fun is that?
Telling people--

290
00:12:29,615 --> 00:12:31,383
-Well, I didn't do it.
-[both chuckle]

291
00:12:31,717 --> 00:12:34,420
I take it as a credit that I possibly
could have... in my own mind.

292
00:12:34,487 --> 00:12:36,722
Well, if you need delusions,
that's your business.

293
00:12:38,791 --> 00:12:41,794
[Jerry] So, when you first started out
in the lounges in Vegas,

294
00:12:41,861 --> 00:12:45,064
-what time was your last show?
-12:00, 2:00, and 5:00 in the morning.

295
00:12:45,131 --> 00:12:46,632
-Three shows a night?
-Three a night.

296
00:12:46,966 --> 00:12:48,866
Over a bar, with guys like this...

297
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:52,966
"Eddie, what is this...
What, is this guy funny?"

298
00:12:53,066 --> 00:12:54,066
It was tough.

299
00:12:54,133 --> 00:12:55,833
-Tough. Huh, jeez.
-Till 5:00?

300
00:12:56,308 --> 00:12:58,544
"Sir...
When you get a minute, could you look up?"

301
00:12:59,666 --> 00:13:03,300
I'm on stage, right?
And it's packed, the lounge.

302
00:13:03,366 --> 00:13:04,599
And the casino's packed,

303
00:13:04,650 --> 00:13:06,418
and it's noisy, no way to stop the noise.

304
00:13:06,852 --> 00:13:10,689
And I'd run out into the casino,
and get on a crap table, and yell,

305
00:13:11,157 --> 00:13:13,092
"All right, that's it!

306
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:17,099
I'm on a goddamn stage and this noise
gotta stop, you understand me?"

307
00:13:17,663 --> 00:13:18,864
I swear, on my father's grave...

308
00:13:20,566 --> 00:13:22,601
["La Virgen de la Macarena" playing]

309
00:13:22,666 --> 00:13:26,066
Who came up with the bullfighter theme
on The Tonight Show

310
00:13:26,133 --> 00:13:27,300
as a play on for you?

311
00:13:27,339 --> 00:13:29,275
-Me. Yeah.
-You did?

312
00:13:29,341 --> 00:13:30,309
Accidentally.

313
00:13:30,376 --> 00:13:33,579
-[cheering, applauding]
-["La Virgen de la Macarena" continues]

314
00:13:33,646 --> 00:13:35,815
-It's me, that's what I do.
-Right.

315
00:13:35,881 --> 00:13:39,485
That's why, when I'd go on a show...
Bob Hope in those days, with the cards...

316
00:13:39,552 --> 00:13:40,519
Mm-hmm.

317
00:13:40,599 --> 00:13:42,433
In the middle of a scene
with George Foreman,

318
00:13:42,500 --> 00:13:45,566
I'd get off the card and say, "George,
stand in the corner, knock yourself out."

319
00:13:45,633 --> 00:13:47,233
Now they couldn't write that.

320
00:13:47,293 --> 00:13:49,929
And the way I say it,
that's not a hilarious thing.

321
00:13:49,995 --> 00:13:52,131
But when I say it, they scream,
because of the way I say it.

322
00:13:53,266 --> 00:13:55,433
-You grew up in New York, right?
-Yes.

323
00:13:55,501 --> 00:13:56,936
Do you have relatives still around?

324
00:13:57,002 --> 00:13:58,003
My mother.

325
00:13:58,070 --> 00:14:00,940
-Your mother's still with us?
-Yeah, 99 this year.

326
00:14:01,006 --> 00:14:02,308
-Oh, my God!
-[pounding]

327
00:14:02,566 --> 00:14:04,266
What am I complaining about? Jesus.

328
00:14:04,310 --> 00:14:06,745
-That's what I'm trying to figure out.
-[laughing]

329
00:14:12,751 --> 00:14:15,287
[Jerry] Nobody wants Don Rickles
for a shrink, by the way.

330
00:14:17,356 --> 00:14:20,492
I'm counting on you, anything we said
that might make somebody look bad,

331
00:14:20,559 --> 00:14:22,361
-I'll kill you.
-I would never do that.

332
00:14:22,428 --> 00:14:24,663
-[Jerry] You think I'm stupid?
-[Don] Yeah.

333
00:14:24,766 --> 00:14:26,000
[both laughing]

334
00:14:32,705 --> 00:14:34,240
...used to do all table stuff.

335
00:14:34,306 --> 00:14:36,208
-You ever hear that name?
-Yeah.

336
00:14:36,300 --> 00:14:38,066
And he would take farina,

337
00:14:38,366 --> 00:14:42,033
put it in his mouth and say,
"Miss, could I have that... [mumbles]"

338
00:14:42,081 --> 00:14:44,283
And he'd let it fall all over the table.

339
00:14:44,599 --> 00:14:46,966
And he was funny as hell.
You got hysterical.

340
00:14:47,019 --> 00:14:49,455
And then, when he went on the stage,
right in the toilet.

341
00:14:49,521 --> 00:14:51,757
He had four, five jokes, and that was it.

342
00:14:51,824 --> 00:14:54,593
But we'd sit in the Cabana,
in the daytime. See, like you,

343
00:14:54,833 --> 00:14:57,500
-pounding the beach chairs on the floor.
-The guy is like that.

344
00:14:57,566 --> 00:14:59,300
-On the floor.
-I know. I know.

345
00:14:59,366 --> 00:15:01,466
We went to the place.

346
00:15:01,533 --> 00:15:03,366
They get up in front of the crowd,
nothing.

347
00:15:03,402 --> 00:15:04,670
Dead. Dead.

348
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:08,674
-Why is that, Don?
-Ah, who knows? Who knows, you know?

349
00:15:08,741 --> 00:15:10,309
Let me take care of this.

350
00:15:10,466 --> 00:15:11,800
No, I insist.

351
00:15:12,111 --> 00:15:14,580
-You see how I was fighting over it?
-[laughing]

